# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_PMU_CONFIGURE */
int64_t pmu_configure(uint32_t tracing, uint32_t op, uint64_t a, uint64_t b);
```

# DESCRIPTION

`pmu_configure` drives the processor's performance monitoring unit: Intel's architectural performance monitoring or AMD's core counters on amd64, PMUv3 on arm64, the SBI PMU on rv64.
<op> picks the operation, <a> and <b> are its arguments, and <tracing> is a resource(2) that grants the TRACING system resource where the operation says so.

An event is a generic one, `CROI_PMU_CYCLES` (0), `CROI_PMU_INSTRUCTIONS` (1), `CROI_PMU_CACHE_MISSES` (2) or `CROI_PMU_BRANCH_MISSES` (3), or the architecture's own event code with `CROI_PMU_RAW` (bit 31) set.

: `CROI_PMU_OP_INFO`
  Writes a `croi_pmu_info_t` to the address <a>: the kind of unit (`CROI_PMU_KIND_NONE`, `_INTEL`, `_AMD`, `_ARM`, `_SBI`), the number of counters the kernel may use, a bitmask of the generic events supported (bit n is event n) and whether overflow sampling is available.
  It needs no resource.
: `CROI_PMU_OP_SAMPLE_START`
  Samples every thread on every CPU: each <b> occurrences of the event <a>, the running thread's program counter and frames are recorded in the trace (trace(2), category `CROI_TRACE_SAMPLE`) with source 1 plus the event, or 0xFF for a raw event.
  It needs the resource.
  <b> must be from 1000 to below half the counter's range, and the event supported (`CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`); a unit that cannot sample is `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.
  It replaces a sampling already on.
: `CROI_PMU_OP_SAMPLE_STOP`
  Stops sampling.
  It needs the resource.
: `CROI_PMU_OP_THREAD_START`
  Counts events for the calling thread, from zero.
  <a> is the number of events, 1 to `CROI_PMU_THREAD_EVENTS` (4) (else `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`), and <b> the address of that many `uint32_t` events.
  It needs no resource.
  A machine with no unit, or an event it lacks, is `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.
  Starting again replaces the counts.
: `CROI_PMU_OP_THREAD_READ`
  Writes four `uint64_t` to the address <a>: the counts so far, in the order of the events given.
  `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` if counting was not started.
: `CROI_PMU_OP_THREAD_STOP`
  Stops counting and discards the counts.

Any other <op> is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.

